%[text] # Detect Anomalies in Time Series Data Using Matrix Profile
%[text] In this example we use distance-based methods to detect anomalies within a long time series signal. Notably, we will not be training a machine learning or deep learning model to do this, but using distance-based pattern matching methods. These methods are very efficient on long time series and do not require any model training.
%[text] *This example requires MATLAB R2026a or later,* [Predictive Maintenance Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/predictive-maintenance.html)*, and the* [Time Series Anomaly Detection for MATLAB](https://www.mathworks.com/products/time-series-anomaly-detection.html) *Support Package.*
%%
%[text] ## Import Data
%[text] The dataset consists of a timetable of armature current measurements of a degrading DC motor. This dataset comes from the example [Motor Current Signature Analysis for Gear Train Fault Detection](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ug/motor-current-signature-analysis-for-gear-train-fault-detection.html). The data also has tachometer pulses, but we won't use that column in this example.
load('servoData.mat', 'faultyData')
%%
%[text] ## Inject Synthetic Anomalies
%[text] Start by injecting a few different [synthetic anomalies](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/predmaint/inject-synthetic-anomalies.html) into the data. In the real world, you won't know where anomalies are located. But for the purposes of an example, it helps to have truth data which allows us to see how well the algorithm detects each anomaly type.
%[text] We will inject three types of anomalies: Drift, shift, and stuck value. Each of these is 25-50 data points long.
data = faultyData(1:5000,"MotorCurrent");

% Set up synthetic anomalies
drift = syntheticAnomaly("Drift",Type="Linear",Scale=0.05);
shift = syntheticAnomaly("Bias",Offset=10);
stuck = syntheticAnomaly("Bias",Offset=0);

% Inject synthetic anomalies
data = injectAnomaly(drift,data,WindowStart=720,WindowLength=25);
data = injectAnomaly(shift,data,WindowStart=3000,WindowLength=50);
data = injectAnomaly(stuck,data,WindowStart=1600,WindowLength=25);

X = data.MotorCurrent;
%%
%[text] ## Set Query Window Length
%[text] Set the query window length to 100, which corresponds to 67 ms of data (the sampling rate is 1500 Hz). The injected anomalies are 25-50 datapoints long. You can experiment with the length of this window. A smaller window will take longer to detect anomalies, but a larger window may not isolate the anomalies finely enough. You will need to understand the approximate time scales of anomalies in your application in order to choose the appropriate window length. This may take some iteration.
len = 100; 
%%
%[text] ## Manually Compare Subsequences Using Distance Profile
%[text] To start, we will use [`distanceProfile`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ref/distanceprofile.html) to compare the query subsequence with all other subsequences in the time series. 
%[text] *Note: To create the* ***interactive*** *distance profile plot, you must run the function below* ***at the command line.***
%[text] Drag the subsequence in yellow to change the query. As you drag, the algorithm compares the query to every other subsequence of the same length in the time series. You will see the motif plotted in green (best match to query), and the discord in purple (worst match to query). 
distanceProfile(X,len) %[output:621e2a35]
%%
%[text] ## Find Top Anomaly Using Matrix Profile
%[text] In many cases you may want to find the most and least similar subsequences in the entire time series -- not just compared to an individual query. This is equivalent to comparing all the motifs and discords for every possible query of the same length, and choosing the most and least similar to the rest of the time series.
%[text] We can use [`matrixProfile`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ref/matrixprofile.html) to do this. The matrix profile of a time series is the vector of minimum z-normalized Euclidean distances between each subsequence and its closest neighbor. If we use this function without output arguments, it will plot the matrix profile and show the top potential anomaly location and the top motif pair (most similar subsequences).
%[text] Note that the top discord in purple corresponds to the highest value of the matrix profile -- that is, the furthest distance from all other subsequences. This corresponds to the location of the stuck value anomaly.
figure %[output:5f767c39]
matrixProfile(X,len) %[output:5f767c39]
%%
%[text] ## Find All Top Anomalies
%[text] Recall that in the beginning of this example we injected 3 different anomalies. How can we find all of them in one go?
%[text] This time, we will use `matrixProfile` with output arguments to return the matrix profile values and indicies. Then, apply [`findDiscord`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ref/finddiscord.html) to identify the top 3 anomalies in the time series. We will say the anomalies need to be at least 200 data points apart to ensure they are different.
numTopAnomalies = 3; 

[MP,MPI] = matrixProfile(X,len);
anomalylocations = findDiscord(MP, MPI, MaxNumDiscords=numTopAnomalies, MinSeparation=200) %[output:65f74de5]
%[text] Recall that the locations of the injected synthetic anomalies were: 
%[text] - **Drift**: 720-745
%[text] - **Shift**: 3000-3050
%[text] - **Stuck Value**: 1600-1625 \
%[text] We can see that the top 3 identified anomalies in `anomalylocations` correspond to each of these anomalies.
%[text] Now, let's plot the locations of these 3 anomalies over the original time series and compare them to the matrix profile plot. We can see that all three anomalies correspond to a peak in the matrix profile.
t = tiledlayout(2,1); %[output:5a8ef895]

ax = nexttile; %[output:5a8ef895]
plot(X) %[output:5a8ef895]
hold(ax, 'on'); %[output:5a8ef895]

% Overlay location of potential anomalies on time series
for k = anomalylocations(:)'
  h = plot(ax, k:k+len, X(k:k+len), LineWidth=1.5, Color=[0.4940 0.1840 0.5560]); %[output:5a8ef895]
end
legend(ax,"Data","Anomaly") %[output:5a8ef895]
title(ax,"Time Series") %[output:5a8ef895]

nexttile %[output:5a8ef895]
findDiscord(MP, MPI, MaxNumDiscords=3, MinSeparation=200); %[output:5a8ef895]
%[text] 

